@NEARProtocol The problem with AI agents holding money:

You give the agent a private key. A vendor's API returns a valid invoice pointing at an attacker's account. The agent pays it — doing its job correctly.

No jailbreak needed. One invoice drains the wallet.

@NEARProtocol Why NEAR specifically:

access keys scope a signer to a receiver + method list at the protocol layer. So the spend limit is enforced by the runtime and the contract — never by the agent's own code.

You can't do that with an EOA + approvals on EVM.

